  "body": {
    "id": "prop:zero-system-sanity",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Fix \\((P,B^0,\\varepsilon,\\pi_0)\\in\\mathfrak M_{p,m}^{\\mathrm{pub}}\\).  If \\(B^{0,(a)}=0\\) for every \\(a\\in M\\), then every compatible order induces the same zero tuple.  For an empty observed view graph, all \\(p!\\) orders are compatible, \\(N_{\\mathrm{out}}=1\\), and the full-lattice fragment algorithm retains exactly one fragment in every subset bucket \\(H_A\\), hence \\(2^p\\) fragments in total.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "B0",
      "epsilon",
      "pi0",
      "M_pub",
      "p",
      "m",
      "a",
      "M",
      "N_out",
      "H_A",
      "A"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:published-limvam-class",
      "def:identified-tuple-set",
      "def:fragment-hashcons-enumerator",
      "ass:centered-gaussian-law",
      "ass:linear-structural-equations",
      "lem:original-row-invariance",
      "thm:published-all-orders-covariance-compatible"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Assume \\(B^{0,(a)}=0\\) for every \\(a\\in M\\).  By\n\\(\\texttt{ass:linear-structural-equations}\\),\n\\(X^{(a)}=\\varepsilon^{(a)}\\).  The within-view product condition in\n\\(\\texttt{ass:centered-gaussian-law}\\) and the positive-variance clause in\n\\(\\texttt{def:published-limvam-class}\\) give a positive diagonal matrix\n\\(\\Sigma^{aa}\\).  Hence, for every \\(A\\subseteq V\\) and \\(r\\notin A\\),\n\\[\n \\Sigma^{aa}[r,A]=0,\n \\qquad \\beta^{(a)}(A,r)=0.\n \\tag{22}\n\\]\nBy \\(\\texttt{lem:original-row-invariance}\\), every order induces the same\nall-zero tuple.  By\n\\(\\texttt{thm:published-all-orders-covariance-compatible}\\), every one of\nthe \\(p!\\) orders is compatible for every \\(G\\), and hence in particular\nwhen \\(E=\\varnothing\\).  It follows that\n\\[\n \\Theta_G(\\mathcal O_G)=\\{0\\},\\qquad N_{\\mathrm{out}}=1.\n\\]\nStarting with the unique empty fragment, every transition in\n\\(\\texttt{def:fragment-hashcons-enumerator}\\) writes a zero row by (22).\nAt any fixed subset all incoming paths therefore produce the same integer\nfragment, which exact trie insertion retains once.  The full lattice has all\n\\(2^p\\) subset buckets, so exactly one fragment is retained in each and\nexactly \\(2^p\\) in total.",
    "justification": "The zero system certifies a genuine factorial-to-exponential collapse of the full-lattice fragment algorithm.",
    "gap": "Order enumeration would still list all \\(p!\\) structural witnesses even though their numerical coefficient output is a singleton.",
    "consumer": "Implementers obtain a boundary regression test for accidental order traversal or failed row interning.",
